00:15:09,160 --> 00:15:15,160
and that really, we really need people in the critical care space that are interested

242
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:18,320
to come in and help with that.

243
00:15:18,320 --> 00:15:25,840
As the class grows, we found that doing these extended simulations can be pretty involved

244
00:15:25,840 --> 00:15:26,840
and take hours.

245
00:15:26,840 --> 00:15:31,360
And so patient, you know, students, we want every student to get through each SIM.

246
00:15:31,360 --> 00:15:34,620
So we would like to have more instructors.

247
00:15:34,620 --> 00:15:37,640
We have a pretty good pool of critical care folks.

248
00:15:37,640 --> 00:15:39,460
We have two PAs that work for us.

249
00:15:39,460 --> 00:15:44,720
We have one PA, special forces medic that just was hired.

250
00:15:44,720 --> 00:15:51,540
We have a couple flight medics from various agencies around.

251
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:53,400
We have yourself.

252
00:15:53,400 --> 00:16:01,000
So we have a pretty good cadre of folks, but always having people to help run these simulations

253
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:04,000
to make sure all the students are getting that hands on is key.

254
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,080
Yeah, that makes sense.

255
00:16:06,080 --> 00:16:11,920
So that's the critical care class that you're doing, but you also do EMT and medic classes

256
00:16:11,920 --> 00:16:12,920
here as well.

257
00:16:12,920 --> 00:16:13,920
Yeah, we do.

258
00:16:13,920 --> 00:16:20,220
So everything from technically EMR all the way up to critical care.

259
00:16:20,220 --> 00:16:27,640
So we offer basic EMT classes or EMT pretty much every semester.

260
00:16:27,640 --> 00:16:34,720
We do advanced EMT and we have a paramedic program that goes, starts every year.

261
00:16:34,720 --> 00:16:36,840
We start a new cohort.

262
00:16:36,840 --> 00:16:40,200
And these all have the option to do for college credit as well?

263
00:16:40,200 --> 00:16:43,500
Yes, our paramedic program, they all are college credit.

264
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:48,060
So everything is towards a degree.

265
00:16:48,060 --> 00:16:52,680
And so people can come in with no EMS background and pursue our paramedicine degree.

266
00:16:52,680 --> 00:16:58,200
So they will start in EMT, they'll take biology, they'll take a couple of their gen ed classes

267
00:16:58,200 --> 00:17:01,880
and then apply to the paramedic program once they get their EMT.

268
00:17:01,880 --> 00:17:07,300
And then they can graduate with their associates degree by the end of the paramedic program.

269
00:17:07,620 --> 00:17:14,660
So our paramedic program is not obviously in the US, we don't require a degree, but

270
00:17:14,660 --> 00:17:21,260
we are moving towards not a degree requirement per se, but we've made our degree program

271
00:17:21,260 --> 00:17:27,180
for our paramedics very streamlined so that they can earn that associates degree by the

272
00:17:27,180 --> 00:17:28,640
time they graduate.

273
00:17:28,640 --> 00:17:32,700
And I would say like majority of our students now pursue that degree.

274
00:17:32,700 --> 00:17:38,060
It's where before when it was like, okay, you had three extra semesters onto your already

275
00:17:38,060 --> 00:17:40,180
four semester long paramedic program.

276
00:17:40,180 --> 00:17:44,860
A lot of people waited to get that degree, maybe waited till later, but we've changed

277
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:46,060
that degree plan a bit.

278
00:17:46,060 --> 00:17:52,340
So someone could come in with no college credit, take the gen ed, maybe some of their gen

279
00:17:52,340 --> 00:17:57,020
ed their first semester and then finish a couple of online classes during the program

280
00:17:57,020 --> 00:17:58,420
and graduate with the degree.

281
00:17:58,420 --> 00:18:00,060
So that's been a big goal.

282
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:01,060
That's awesome.

283
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:06,500
And I've been out of that space for a while, but I imagine it's fairly different than how

284
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:10,580
most paramedic programs across the country are doing it these days.

285
00:18:10,580 --> 00:18:12,420
Yeah.

286
00:18:12,420 --> 00:18:20,100
The difference, the disparity between paramedic programs is pretty vast in the US.

287
00:18:20,100 --> 00:18:27,180
It's like, so since 2013, every paramedic program in the country has to be what's called

288
00:18:27,180 --> 00:18:28,660
K-HEP accredited.

289
00:18:28,660 --> 00:18:31,540
And that started because it, K-HEP has been around.

290
00:18:31,540 --> 00:18:36,020
Paramedic programs have been accredited for a very long time, but it wasn't a requirement.

291
00:18:36,020 --> 00:18:40,260
And in 2013, the national registry said, if you're going to take the national exam, you

292
00:18:40,260 --> 00:18:43,380
had to have gone to a K-HEP accredited program.

293
00:18:43,380 --> 00:18:45,140
So that pretty much sealed the deal.

294
00:18:45,140 --> 00:18:48,620
Now all 50 states utilize the national registry.

295
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:54,220
So every paramedic program for the most part, unless there's some states that still recognize

296
00:18:54,220 --> 00:18:59,300
a state paramedic, all have a K-HEP accreditation.

297
00:18:59,300 --> 00:19:06,780
And so part of that is like, if you are going to a paramedic school, you have to be awarded

298
00:19:06,780 --> 00:19:10,260
college credit by an institution.

299
00:19:10,260 --> 00:19:14,020
Doesn't necessarily have to be by the paramedic program, but the program itself has to be

300
00:19:14,020 --> 00:19:18,140
sponsored by an institution that would give college credit for that.

301
00:19:18,140 --> 00:19:19,980
And it has to be towards degree completion.

302
00:19:19,980 --> 00:19:25,620
It couldn't just be like, we're going to give you 50 credit hours of whatever elective

303
00:19:25,620 --> 00:19:31,260
and welding or I don't know, it has to be towards degree completion of something.

304
00:19:31,260 --> 00:19:37,060
And so pretty much now every paramedic program, every paramedic student has the option to

305
00:19:37,060 --> 00:19:42,540
get a degree, at least an associates degree.

306
00:19:42,540 --> 00:19:47,740
It's just how we get there in terms of making that a requirement is a different, a little

307
00:19:47,740 --> 00:19:49,260
bit different of a discussion.

308
00:19:49,260 --> 00:19:53,980
But I think our model is pretty good because a student can come in and not spend much extra

309
00:19:53,980 --> 00:19:59,100
time in school and get that college degree and really open up a lot more doors, give

310
00:19:59,100 --> 00:20:04,700
themselves a lot more bigger jumping board, so to speak, to go to different things and

311
00:20:04,700 --> 00:20:07,520
expand their career.

312
00:20:07,520 --> 00:20:12,420
And I think New Mexico does a good job because we are like, we have six paramedic programs

313
00:20:12,420 --> 00:20:15,520
and they all offer a degree and one of them is a four year degree.

314
00:20:15,520 --> 00:20:20,040
So I think we're pretty unique in that aspect.

315
00:20:20,040 --> 00:20:25,480
Do you know offhand how, like what percentage of your students who finish the paramedic

316
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:28,400
portion get a degree?

317
00:20:28,400 --> 00:20:35,700
I don't know the exact percentage, but I know since we've updated our degree plan like this

318
00:20:35,700 --> 00:20:42,300
current cohort, I think everybody in this cohort is pursuing that degree.

319
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:52,460
So that has been a big, big, big benefit for us to see that growth where before it

320
00:20:52,460 --> 00:20:59,940
was probably in the 30% because it was such an extra add on of time.

321
00:20:59,940 --> 00:21:04,140
And if you're trying to get out and work and you don't want to invest that extra time,

322
00:21:04,140 --> 00:21:11,020
I do understand it's kind of, you know, it's not typical to spend seven semesters in school

323
00:21:11,020 --> 00:21:14,400
to get a, an associates degree.

324
00:21:14,400 --> 00:21:21,320
And our paramedic students were, when we calculated it compared to a nursing degree had somewhere

325
00:21:21,320 --> 00:21:27,400
in the ballpark of 75 to 85 credit hours in a nursing degree, it was like 69 credit

326
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:31,140
hours and they're spending about the same amount of time in school.

327
00:21:31,140 --> 00:21:36,280
So we really wanted to make sure we were like better accounting for those, those hours.

328
00:21:36,280 --> 00:21:37,280
Right on.

329
00:21:37,280 --> 00:21:38,280
Yeah.

330
00:21:38,280 --> 00:21:44,740
And you mentioned that all the paramedic programs in the state now, all of them offer

331
00:21:44,740 --> 00:21:46,220
like a similar pathway.

332
00:21:46,220 --> 00:21:47,220
Yep.

333
00:21:47,220 --> 00:21:53,300
Is there anything specific in like how things are done here in New Mexico or like the challenges

334
00:21:53,300 --> 00:21:58,300
to EMS education that are specific to our state that you guys are trying to work on

335
00:21:58,300 --> 00:22:00,940
at the moment?

336
00:22:00,940 --> 00:22:07,440
There are, the state has a lot of challenges in healthcare in general, recruiting providers.

337
00:22:07,460 --> 00:22:12,940
And so we, we have shortages just like every other, every other field, but the paramedic

338
00:22:12,940 --> 00:22:15,000
shortages is pretty profound.

339
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:22,260
We just did a big survey across every survey, every survey across every service in New Mexico,

340
00:22:22,260 --> 00:22:27,440
fire service, third service, hospital, private flight services.

341
00:22:27,440 --> 00:22:32,620
And a lot of different areas in our state are pretty understaffed when it comes to paramedics.

342
00:22:32,620 --> 00:22:34,620
And that's true across the country.

343
00:22:34,640 --> 00:22:40,440
And I think when you look at that, people start saying, well, what are the, where are

344
00:22:40,440 --> 00:22:41,920
those challenges coming from?

345
00:22:41,920 --> 00:22:46,160
And the, an easy solution is to be like, well, the school's too hard.

346
00:22:46,160 --> 00:22:47,160
The school's too long.

347
00:22:47,160 --> 00:22:52,160
They have to take this validated national exam and it's challenging and some people

348
00:22:52,160 --> 00:22:53,360
don't pass it.

349
00:22:53,360 --> 00:22:58,440
And some of those things are looked at as like barriers to fixing staffing problems.

350
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:03,240
And so I think every paramedic program faces like some of those where they're like trying

351
00:23:03,240 --> 00:23:09,420
to stand up to this idea that education is causing staffing problems.

352
00:23:09,420 --> 00:23:15,360
I'm not saying that that's the sentiment here in this state, but we definitely face staffing

353
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:18,980
issues and staffing shortages.

354
00:23:18,980 --> 00:23:24,780
And there's a lot of discussions about how we should go about fixing that.

355
00:23:24,780 --> 00:23:29,340
And I think one of the ways we want to fix that is not, is by recruiting students into

356
00:23:29,340 --> 00:23:36,400
our program and by not necessarily by increasing our standards by, and that may be counterintuitive,

357
00:23:36,400 --> 00:23:40,840
but we want to be the program that's looked at as, you know, that's going to, where students

358
00:23:40,840 --> 00:23:43,640
can come and learn more, do expanded clinicals.

359
00:23:43,640 --> 00:23:47,480
Like, you know, our students get a lot of clinicals that are above and beyond what a

360
00:23:47,480 --> 00:23:48,720
typical paramedic does.

361
00:23:48,720 --> 00:23:51,400
I mean, they're certified in ultrasound.

362
00:23:51,400 --> 00:23:59,900
When they graduate, they do multiple rotations with sonographers and doing ultrasounds in

363
00:23:59,900 --> 00:24:00,900
the ER.

364
00:24:00,900 --> 00:24:06,700
So they're pretty expert in like leading kind of technologies in the EMS world.

365
00:24:06,700 --> 00:24:11,460
And that's what we want to utilize to like draw people into the profession of like, look,

366
00:24:11,460 --> 00:24:16,740
there is these great clinical opportunities and this is a really rewarding job instead

367
00:24:16,740 --> 00:24:21,080
of, all right, we're going to just teach you the bare bones and get you through a program

368
00:24:21,080 --> 00:24:25,760
in six state, nine months and get you out in the field and then you'll quit two years

369
00:24:25,760 --> 00:24:26,760
later.

370
00:24:26,760 --> 00:24:30,960
So we want to make people interested in becoming paramedics.

371
00:24:30,960 --> 00:24:36,480
And we also want to make sure that we are trying to take in as many students as we can.

372
00:24:36,480 --> 00:24:39,120
So that's a huge challenge.

373
00:24:39,120 --> 00:24:40,760
Like are there enough clinical sites?

374
00:24:40,760 --> 00:24:45,320
Are there enough internships for patients or students to go to?

375
00:24:45,320 --> 00:24:47,740
So that's something we run into.

376
00:24:48,380 --> 00:24:54,180
We only historically have accepted only 15 students into each cohort and we're trying

377
00:24:54,180 --> 00:24:58,500
to up that number so that we can help alleviate some of these shortages.

378
00:24:58,500 --> 00:24:59,500
Yeah.

379
00:24:59,500 --> 00:25:07,420
And you mentioned that you guys are including ultrasound training in your initial paramedic

380
00:25:07,420 --> 00:25:08,420
courses.

381
00:25:08,420 --> 00:25:09,420
Yeah.

382
00:25:09,420 --> 00:25:10,420
Yeah, I think that's cool.

383
00:25:10,420 --> 00:25:15,700
And we've also seen, you and I were just talking about this, a lot of the ground services

384
00:25:15,700 --> 00:25:18,900
in our area are starting to introduce that as well.

385
00:25:18,900 --> 00:25:25,660
Whereas traditionally it's been something that more senior providers removed from school,

386
00:25:25,660 --> 00:25:27,540
like take that on.

387
00:25:27,540 --> 00:25:31,580
Yeah, I think so.

388
00:25:31,580 --> 00:25:37,420
I've seen the utility of ultrasound in my own practice and I was not one of these people

389
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:40,480
that was like super bought into it initially.

390
00:25:40,480 --> 00:25:46,640
But after seeing in a variety of different realms, I mean, from working in a critical

391
00:25:46,640 --> 00:25:54,440
care transport world and helping guide treatment decisions to working at ski patrol and detecting

392
00:25:54,440 --> 00:25:59,080
a tension pneumothorax after a tree strike.

393
00:25:59,080 --> 00:26:05,760
I've seen this tool that's quite simple actually to teach somebody some basic skills and really

394
00:26:05,760 --> 00:26:07,680
make a big difference in practice.

395
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:14,040
And so we wanted to bring that into the paramedic world and kind of model a little bit more

396
00:26:14,040 --> 00:26:19,920
how like a physician would be trained in medical school to use ultrasound and it's to incorporate

397
00:26:19,920 --> 00:26:24,920
it into their assessment and to utilize those findings just as like more evidence.

398
00:26:24,920 --> 00:26:28,580
And we also use it to teach the anatomy and physiology.

399
00:26:28,580 --> 00:26:33,240
So they start during their anatomy and physiology, which is their entire first semester.

400
00:26:33,240 --> 00:26:40,440
They're identifying like gallbladder, liver, kidney, renal pyramids, different vessels,

401
00:26:40,440 --> 00:26:45,640
aorta, IVC, hepatic vessels, structures in the lung.

402
00:26:45,640 --> 00:26:50,400
They look at heart valves, they're labeling all these things in their final, in A&P, their

403
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:55,320
lab final is to like, they have a list of structures and they have to find it with ultrasound

404
00:26:55,320 --> 00:26:56,320
and label it.

405
00:26:56,320 --> 00:26:58,280
So that's kind of how we introduce it.

406
00:26:58,280 --> 00:27:01,280
We don't really teach any findings.

407
00:27:01,280 --> 00:27:04,840
We're just learning how to find stuff and point it out.

408
00:27:04,840 --> 00:27:06,760
And then each block we introduce things.

409
00:27:06,760 --> 00:27:11,260
So like in respiratory, they learn all the basic assessments that a paramedic student

410
00:27:11,260 --> 00:27:14,960
needs to learn, but then they're going to learn, you know, to look for lung sliding,

411
00:27:14,960 --> 00:27:20,560
what a consolidation looks like, what B lines look like, what a pleural effusion looks like

412
00:27:20,560 --> 00:27:22,500
and how that would present.

413
00:27:22,500 --> 00:27:27,480
And then in cardiology, we teach them how to look for estimate ejection fraction and

414
00:27:27,480 --> 00:27:36,640
look for like signs of infarct or ventricular strain or mitral regurgitation and as well

415
00:27:36,640 --> 00:27:40,960
as like aortic assessment and IVC assessment.

416
00:27:40,960 --> 00:27:46,120
They start incorporating this into scenarios and then in their medical block, they kind

417
00:27:46,120 --> 00:27:50,760
of learn more like the rush, how to differentiate hypotension and trauma.

418
00:27:50,760 --> 00:27:53,840
Obviously we teach them the fast and then this all kind of culminates and then they

419
00:27:53,840 --> 00:27:57,460
go to clinicals and they get to actually do these on patients and then they spend time

420
00:27:57,460 --> 00:27:58,900
with a sonographer.

421
00:27:58,900 --> 00:27:59,900
So it's pretty robust.

422
00:27:59,900 --> 00:28:04,640
I mean, it's like, it's, it's a over 15 months.

423
00:28:04,640 --> 00:28:05,940
They get quite a bit of hours.

424
00:28:05,940 --> 00:28:06,940
Yeah.

425
00:28:06,940 --> 00:28:10,180
So throughout the entire course, they're developing the skillset, which I think is awesome.

426
00:28:10,180 --> 00:28:11,180
Yeah.

427
00:28:11,180 --> 00:28:12,700
And I think really that's how it needs to be.

428
00:28:12,700 --> 00:28:17,500
I think this, like, I've seen a lot of rollouts of ultrasound and agencies where they do like

429
00:28:17,500 --> 00:28:22,800
a 30 minute training and then it just sits in the ambulance and never gets used because

430
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:25,320
everyone's like, I don't know that training.

431
00:28:25,320 --> 00:28:26,720
I didn't really feel super comfortable.

432
00:28:26,720 --> 00:28:27,720
For sure.

433
00:28:27,720 --> 00:28:29,280
Like, I don't even know if I can find the structure I'm looking for.

434
00:28:29,280 --> 00:28:30,760
How do I even turn it on?

435
00:28:30,760 --> 00:28:31,760
Yeah.

436
00:28:31,760 --> 00:28:32,760
Like, so yeah.

437
00:28:32,760 --> 00:28:35,860
And if you don't have confidence in like what you're doing, like to look for something or

438
00:28:35,860 --> 00:28:40,000
what you're looking at, you're probably not going to adopt that into your practice.

439
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:47,360
So we wanted to, essentially the goal is these people can go out and train and like behind

440
00:28:47,360 --> 00:28:53,020
you is one of our students from the Apache Nation, last cohort, he put together this

441
00:28:53,020 --> 00:29:00,680
whole research project on how to train every, all the medics up in the Hickory Apache Nation

442
00:29:00,680 --> 00:29:02,280
to use ultrasound.

443
00:29:02,280 --> 00:29:05,880
And it's something now they're incorporating and it's a great tool for them because they're

444
00:29:05,880 --> 00:29:09,720
sometimes four hours from the nearest resource.

445
00:29:09,720 --> 00:29:14,200
So they can really get a lot more information.

446
00:29:14,200 --> 00:29:17,480
So yeah, it's been pretty cool to see.

447
00:29:17,480 --> 00:29:18,760
Yeah, it's awesome.

448
00:29:18,760 --> 00:29:19,760
Yeah.

449
00:29:19,760 --> 00:29:23,440
Do you know of any other programs, any other medic programs that are doing something similar

450
00:29:23,440 --> 00:29:24,600
in their initial training?

451
00:29:24,600 --> 00:29:32,600
I don't know if anybody's fully gone in as much as we have into it, but I know there

452
00:29:32,600 --> 00:29:37,680
are a lot of programs that definitely have ultrasounds and maybe incorporate it into

453
00:29:37,680 --> 00:29:40,720
some extent into their programs.

454
00:29:40,720 --> 00:29:44,520
I know like EKU utilizes it.

455
00:29:44,520 --> 00:29:53,280
I think Oklahoma City or one of the programs out there is utilizing it, but I think in

456
00:29:53,280 --> 00:30:00,660
terms of really incorporating it into different aspects of the program, we've gone pretty

457
00:30:00,660 --> 00:30:02,000
full in on that.

458
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,800
Yeah, that seems like it.

459
00:30:04,800 --> 00:30:07,360
And then maybe not to this extreme, but is there anything else that you're trying to

460
00:30:07,360 --> 00:30:13,960
do in your EMS education stuff here at Santa Fe Community College that you think is different

461
00:30:13,960 --> 00:30:16,520
than how most folks are doing it?

462
00:30:16,520 --> 00:30:22,240
Yeah, I mean, it's just, I think we don't treat people like paramedics, right?

463
00:30:22,240 --> 00:30:24,840
You're going to be an advanced clinician of some kind.

464
00:30:24,840 --> 00:30:26,360
And that's sort of how I look at it.

465
00:30:26,360 --> 00:30:34,600
It's this idea of like, we're training you to be just working this emergency medical

466
00:30:34,600 --> 00:30:36,280
transport mindset.

467
00:30:36,280 --> 00:30:43,800
But we kind of are training people to be just give them a broader idea of being a healthcare

468
00:30:43,800 --> 00:30:50,320
practitioner, so to speak, and a better idea of the healthcare system and how to navigate

469
00:30:50,320 --> 00:30:55,440
different aspects of the healthcare system to have a deeper understanding of what is

470
00:30:55,440 --> 00:31:00,200
going to happen with their patient further down the line in their care.

471
00:31:00,200 --> 00:31:06,240
So there's an expectation of like our paramedic students to understand lab values, to understand

472
00:31:06,240 --> 00:31:11,120
blood gases, to understand all the different classes of home medications when we're talking

